R {968}{0}{}{Rapier grass (w,s)}{ }{The lush reeds have given out to Rapier grass here, where the ground is drier. The grass grows tall and thickly all the way up to the base of the limestone cliffs. A patch of the tough, sharp-edged grass has been cropped almost clear to the ground. }{Valley of the Tribes}{}{}{}{1.000}{1}
E {967}{w}{w}{8}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}
E {969}{s}{s}{4}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}

R {969}{0}{}{Reeds (w,s,n)}{ }{The reeds grow uninterrupted in all directions except to the east where the limestone cliffs have halted their advance. The recent rains have torn large chunks off the cliff face and lumps of limestone are embedded among the reeds. The rich mud sucks at your feet and makes walking difficult. }{Valley of the Tribes}{}{}{}{1.000}{1}
E {968}{n}{n}{1}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}
E {970}{s}{s}{4}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}
E {977}{w}{w}{8}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}

R {970}{0}{}{Reeds (sw,w,n)}{ }{The valley spreads out into a plain of reeds which becomes submerged when the river overflows during the seasonal rains. Stands of flood-  resistant bamboo dot the plains. Grayish limestone cliffs soar skywards in the north and block your way east. The ground slopes gradually upwards to the south and west. }{Valley of the Tribes}{}{}{}{1.000}{1}
E {969}{n}{n}{1}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}
E {971}{sw}{sw}{12}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}
