R {4206}{0}{}{View of San Diego (ne,exit,n)}{<128>X<088>}{San Diego... drink it in. It always goes down smooth. It's a fact; it's the greatest city in the history of mankind. Discovered in 1904, they named it San Diego, which of course in German means a whale�s vagina. A ramp leads toward a bridge over the bay to the north while a small path meanders through the park to the northeast. There's also a phone booth off to the side of the road. }{Anchorman}{anchorman}{}{}{1.000}{1}
E {4205}{exit}{exit}{0}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}
E {4207}{ne}{ne}{3}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}
E {4208}{n}{n}{1}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}

R {4207}{0}{}{Path through the park (sw)}{ }{Palm trees line the freshly mowed grass of this park while a gleaming white sidewalk cuts through it toward the blue water of the bay. A few birds can be seen flying over the bay in the distance. Strangely, the Channel 9 news van is parked beside the sidewalk, marring the otherwise peaceful surroundings. }{Anchorman}{}{}{}{1.000}{1}
E {4206}{sw}{sw}{12}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}

R {4208}{0}{}{A bridge over the bay (n,s)}{ }{This four lane bridge spans the bay, leading in and out of San Diego. Street lights line the edge of the bridge along with a guard rail to prevent anyone from falling over the side. Even with this precaution, horrific events have been known to happen along this span. Just the other day there was a motorcycle accident and a case of dog punting. Truly tragic. }{Anchorman}{}{}{}{1.000}{1}
E {4206}{s}{s}{4}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}
E {4209}{n}{n}{1}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}

R {4209}{0}{}{Main Street (w,s,n)}{ }{Palm trees rise above colorful gardens planted along the sides of the street. Parallel parking is available in front of the gardens. The meters stand ready and waiting while pedestrians wander back and forth along the sidewalk. The offices along this street all look well-preserved and the street itself is remarkably clean. }{Anchorman}{}{}{}{1.000}{1}
E {4208}{s}{s}{4}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}
E {4210}{w}{w}{8}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}
E {4211}{n}{n}{1}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}

R {4210}{0}{}{The San Diego Zoo (e)}{ }{The San Diego Zoo is one of the best zoos in the country. Today is a special day here at the zoo since the world's most famous panda, Ling Wong, is about to give birth. People are crowded all around the panda exhibit, just waiting on the big event. Luckily, there's railing along the fake stacked stone to keep people from falling inside the exhibit and getting eaten alive by bears. }{Anchorman}{}{}{}{1.000}{1}
E {4209}{e}{e}{2}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}

R {4211}{0}{}{Main Street (e,s,n)}{ }{Palm trees line this busy boulevard, their fronds swaying gently in the Southern California breeze. One story office fronts stand on both sides of the street and an alley cuts between them to the east. Warm sunlight bakes the street and you. Perhaps you should wander inside at some point and cool off. }{Anchorman}{}{}{}{1.000}{1}
E {4209}{s}{s}{4}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}
E {4212}{e}{e}{2}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}
E {4213}{n}{n}{1}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}

R {4212}{0}{}{Back Alley (w)}{ }{This alley sits beneath a bridge and behind a series of brick buildings. It looks like it may have served as a parking lot at some point, but now it seems better suited to hosting vicious cock fights. There are some blood stains on the pavement and some old crates stacked in front of one of the loading docks. }{Anchorman}{}{}{}{1.000}{1}
E {4211}{w}{w}{8}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}

R {4213}{0}{}{Main Street (enter,w,s)}{<168>E<088>}{There was a time, a time before cable, when the local anchorman reigned supreme. When people believed everything they heard on tv. This was an age when only men were allowed to read the news and in San Diego, one anchorman was more man than the rest. That man works in the building across the street at Channel 4. That man is Ron Burgundy. There's a large billboard just atop the building and a side street off to the west. }{Anchorman}{}{}{}{1.000}{1}
E {4211}{s}{s}{4}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}
E {4214}{w}{w}{8}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}
E {4216}{enter}{enter}{0}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}
E {4216}{n}{enter}{1}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}

R {4214}{0}{}{Side Street (w,e)}{ }{This residential side street is lined with ranch-style houses owned by some of San Diego's elite citizens. Every lawn is perfectly manicured and the sidewalks that stretch along the street look freshly swept and immaculately paved. A tricycle sits on one driveway and a red ribbon is tied around a mailbox at the western end of the cul-de-sac. }{Anchorman}{}{}{}{1.000}{1}
E {4213}{e}{e}{2}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}
E {4215}{w}{w}{8}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}

R {4215}{0}{}{Party House (e)}{ }{This house looks like a cyclone hit it. Clearly, a major party went down here last night and no one has been around to clean up after it yet. Beer bottles, plastic cups, and wine glasses are all over the place and there are multiple stains on the shag carpet. Outside the glass patio doors is the pool area which looks even worse. Most of the lawn furniture is in the pool and half the colored lights strung out across the lawn are broken. }{Anchorman}{}{}{}{1.000}{1}
E {4214}{e}{e}{2}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}

R {4216}{0}{}{The Lobby (e,s,n)}{ }{This is the lobby of the Channel 4 building. Large glass doors lead back outside while inside, the wood paneled walls display giant framed posters of the channel's newscasters. A receptionist's desk sits just inside the entrance, but no one is sitting there at the moment. A blue fabric-covered chair sits empty behind the desk and a flower arrangement sits atop one corner of the desk. }{Anchorman}{}{}{}{1.000}{1}
E {4217}{e}{e}{2}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}
E {4221}{n}{n}{1}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}
E {4213}{s}{s}{4}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}

R {4217}{0}{}{The Bullpen (e,w,se,u)}{ }{If you work at Channel 4, but don't warrant your own office, this is where you find a desk. The furniture is a uniform lime green and the  walls are painted a garish orange. A television and vcr sit on a metal stand on the far end of the room for screening purposes and a series of monitors take up one wall so workers can keep an eye on their competition. A wide staircase leads upstairs. }{Anchorman}{}{}{}{1.000}{1}
E {4216}{w}{w}{8}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}
E {4218}{se}{se}{6}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}
E {4219}{e}{e}{2}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}
E {4220}{u}{u}{16}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}

R {4218}{0}{}{Brian's Office (nw)}{ }{This is the business address of roaving reporter, Brian Fantana. The desk and chair are pretty standard, but the most impressive feature of the office are the floor-to-ceiling bookshelves that line one wall. Why would bookshelves be impressive? Because one of them opens up to reveal a giant display case full of men's cologne. It never ceases to amaze. When it's time to musk up, Brian has his choice of scents including favorites like London Gentleman and Blackbeard's Delight. }{Anchorman}{}{}{}{1.000}{1}
E {4217}{nw}{nw}{9}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}

R {4219}{0}{}{Ron's Office (w)}{ }{This is the office of the legendary Ron Burgundy. It has rich wood paneling and a floor-to-ceiling window that provides a view of downtown San Diego. A shelf holds mementos of Ron's career including one of his five prized Emmys. On his desk sits a pile of paperwork, an open bottle of beer, and a half-empty pint of milk. }{Anchorman}{}{}{}{1.000}{1}
E {4217}{w}{w}{8}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}

R {4220}{0}{}{Ed's Office (d)}{ }{This is the office of station manager, Ed Harken. Mini-blinds on the windows offer privacy when Ed has to hold meetings or wants to do some serious drinking at the mini-bar across from his desk. A large bookshelf holds various tomes related to business and broadcasting and oddly enough, there's a slot machine in the corner. }{Anchorman}{}{}{}{1.000}{1}
E {4217}{d}{d}{32}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}

R {4221}{0}{}{The Newsroom (w,s,n)}{ }{The Channel 4 newsroom. This is where the magic happens, folks. The news desk stretches around the orange and beige set with room for five members of the team to make their reports. Three large screens on the walls behind the desk are used for displaying visuals and a trio of cameras are parked across from the desk, all set up and ready to go for when the station manager yells 'Action!' }{Anchorman}{}{}{}{1.000}{1}
E {4216}{s}{s}{4}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}
E {4222}{w}{w}{8}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}
E {4223}{n}{n}{1}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}

R {4222}{0}{}{Control Room (e)}{ }{This is the control room. It allows the crew to monitor the broadcast and control which cameras are live and what visuals are displayed on the screens in the newsroom next door. A microphone and a stack of tapes sit on the desk. The monitors are currently displaying nothing but a multi-colored test since there's not a broadcast underway at the moment. }{Anchorman}{}{}{}{1.000}{1}
E {4221}{e}{e}{2}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}

R {4223}{0}{}{Backstage (s)}{ }{Just before they go on the air, you can usually find the cast back here getting prepped with hair, wardrobe, and make-up. Although Ron has accused the make-up artists of being 'bush league' on more than one occasion, they usually do a pretty good job. Several chairs sit in front of well-lit mirrors, ready and waiting for the next broadcast. }{Anchorman}{brick}{}{}{1.000}{1}
E {4221}{s}{s}{4}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}

