R {7888}{0}{<238>}{Passage (s)}{<238>] [<088>}{This wide tunnel is capable of supporting quite a bit of foot traffic if the need arose.  The passage is lit by neither torch nor visible magic.  Marble-like veins of luminescent rock embedded in the walls glow brightly enough to illuminate your surroundings.  This area seems to house dignitaries and servants, though you note no signs of true royalty. }{Grand Hall of the Rohm Giddor}{}{}{}{1.000}{1}
E {7887}{s}{s}{4}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}

R {7889}{0}{<248>}{Banquet (sw,se)}{<248>[ ]<088>}{This large room is roughly rectangular in shape, though the long walls to the north and south bow out a bit toward their centers.  A broad and sturdy wooden table fills the center of the room.  It is currently empty, but the room could easily hold two hundred guests.  Heavy wooden chairs are pulled back from the table, and the floor beneath is wet in places.  Above the table, three iron chandeliers hang suspended by heavy chain.  A wide archway opens to a passage to the southeast and a draped doorway lies to the southwest. }{Grand Hall of the Rohm Giddor}{}{}{}{1.000}{1}
E {7878}{se}{se}{6}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}
E {7890}{sw}{sw}{12}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}

R {7890}{0}{<248>}{Kitchen (n,ne)}{<248>.<018>#<248>.<088>}{This may very well be the largest kitchen your eyes have seen.  The center of the room is dominated by chopping blocks and other surfaces for preparation, along with some wood-burning iron stoves, none of which are currently heated.  The western portion of the room serves to hold stocks of grains and preserved foods at the ready.  Four open hearths take up the south wall, the maintenance fires within dwindling low.  Smaller holes in the wall, close enough to be heated by the fiery hearths, serve as bread ovens.  A doorway to the northeast leads back to the banquet hall and double swinging doors lead north to another room. }{Grand Hall of the Rohm Giddor}{}{}{}{1.000}{1}
E {7889}{ne}{ne}{3}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}
E {7891}{n}{n}{1}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}

R {7891}{0}{<248>}{Scullery (s)}{<248>[ ]<088>}{You are now within a dimly lit but impeccably clean scullery.  Three large basins, two full of water, sit in front of the north wall.  Lining the east and west walls are doorless cabinets that are stacked neatly with all sorts and sizes of plates and dinnerware.  Mops, buckets and other cleaning supplies are cluttered together to the right of the basins.  The only exit is back south through the swinging doors. }{Grand Hall of the Rohm Giddor}{}{}{}{1.000}{1}
E {7890}{s}{s}{4}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}

R {7892}{0}{<238>}{Tunnel (n)}{<238>] [<088>}{This section of tunnel differs drastically from previous tunnels. It is very narrow, barely allowing two average-size humans to walk abreast, and were they to do so, they would be constantly scraping their shoulders against the sharp edges of the rocky outcroppings left by the excavators. Signs of battle and more scorch marks pervade the passage, and to the south a cave-in has completely blocked off the tunnel. }{Tunnels of the Rohm Giddor}{}{}{}{1.000}{1}
E {7858}{n}{n}{1}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}

R {7893}{0}{<238>}{Shelf (nw,s)}{<238>^ ^<088>}{This shelf of rock, several paces in width, acts as a walkway along the river as it flows south. Here the cavern's northward passage ends, though there is a smaller tunnel leading northwest. To the east the river enters the cavern, flowing south and under the bridge which can barely be seen through the darkness. }{Tunnels of the Rohm Giddor}{}{}{}{1.000}{1}
