R {20163}{0}{}{Entrance to Lemuria (enter)}{ }{Waking as if from one dream into another, you gaze upon a wonderous sight.  Before you is a sprawling, vibrant city. You can smell the salty scent of the sea surrounding you. Copper skinned people walk past you, tending to their shops, hawking their own wares and sampling those of others.  The voice of the old man comes to you as clear as if you were in the rickety old cabin with him.  'Here I begin my tale of Lemuria... a city which was destroyed by the forces of Kayos and the power lust of the dark mages. What you see is merely the reflection that I paint for you of this once great empire... please explore... and mourn its loss.' }{Lemuria Dream}{}{}{}{1.000}{1}
E {20169}{enter}{enter}{0}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}
E {20169}{se}{enter}{6}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}

R {20164}{0}{<238>}{Spring of Kierlanai (sw,n)}{<238>.^.<088>}{The old man says: "Moss had collected on the outer edge of the    eastern half of the boardwalk. The first two times I went on    pilgrimage, I took the eastern path. I felt that the safety    of it, compared to the dangerous west side, was a sign of    welcome and peace. It showed that I was there for knowledge    and wonder, and not to make grandiose requests, as some did." }{Spring}{}{}{}{1.000}{1}
E {20162}{sw}{sw}{12}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}
E {20161}{n}{n}{1}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}

R {20165}{0}{<268>}{Spring of Kierlanai (n,se,sw)}{<268>~ ~<088>}{The old man says: "Here knelt the spring statue, the giver    of the water. Her hand outstretched, its back laying upon    the ground, the water issued forth from her palm. I can    recall it so clearly, even now...." }{Spring}{}{}{}{1.000}{1}
E {20161}{se}{se}{6}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}
E {20166}{n}{n}{1}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}
E {20167}{sw}{sw}{12}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}

R {20166}{0}{<178>}{Spring of Kierlanai (s)}{<158>*<168>*<138>*<088>}{The old man whispers: "Oh, tranquillity divine. The trees that    had been planted along the north edge of the summit were    gradually groomed over time to form a natural arch over    the statue. She stood on block of marble, nestled eons ago    into the mountain itself, by whatever strange power put her    there. It was like being before the throne of the heavens    with the mother of creation." }{Spring}{}{}{}{1.000}{1}
E {20165}{s}{s}{4}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}

R {20167}{0}{<238>}{Spring of Kierlanai (s,ne)}{<238>===<088>}{The old man says: "The west side of the path was a bit more    treacherous than the east. The boardwalk lay closer to the    edge on that side. One time, I felt it ease slightly under    my steps, and a small rock tumbled down the mountainside.    My heart beat faster, but I felt that it helped me appreciate    the journey even more, and to reflect upon the power." }{Spring}{}{}{}{1.000}{1}
E {20165}{ne}{ne}{3}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}
E {20168}{s}{s}{4}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}

R {20168}{0}{<238>}{Spring of Kierlanai (bridge,n)}{<238>===<088>}{The old man says: "Across the bridge was one step closer to the    spring and the statues. I remember my first tentative steps    across that bridge... I travelled to the Spring of Kierlanai    three times, and it wasn't until the third that I could do it.    There was such power and history there that I did not feel at    all worthy to walk over it, as though I were pronouncing my    superiority... And we both know the sad path it takes us." }{Spring}{}{}{}{1.000}{1}
E {20167}{n}{n}{1}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}
E {20162}{se}{bridge}{6}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}
