R {20346}{0}{<278>}{-=\x7B* Torchstroll Way *\x7D=- (nw,e)}{<278> +-<088>}{The dense forest to the northeast comes almost to the road, with white stone homes and buildings choked with trees and thick vines barely holding it at bay.  To the northwest is a bridge, while directly west, ruined buildings stand on the high banks above a stream.  It is deadly silent and not a living thing can be seen.  To the sides of the road, grander buildings in disrepair amongst the rubble pay silent testimony to the dreadful devastation that took place here.  Stepping quietly on the cracked bricks of this road you listen for signs of life and hardly surprisingly, you detect none at all.  Any living thing with any common sense avoids this place. }{Murus Faralain}{}{}{}{1.000}{1}
E {20347}{e}{e}{2}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}
E {20348}{nw}{nw}{9}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}

R {20347}{0}{<278>}{-=\x7B* Torchstroll Way *\x7D=- (se,w)}{<278>-+ <088>}{On either side of the road, multi-story buildings and offices with some holes in their roofs wait quietly for you to explore.  It is almost perfectly clear of vegetation.  The dense forest to the northeast comes almost to the road, with white stone homes and buildings choked with trees and thick vines barely holding it at bay.  To the south, more open parkland dotted with ruined buildings indicates that this was once an affluent part of the city.  A small movement in some shrubs in the distance catches your eye.  You pause in the middle of the road and wish you could just disappear into the worn pavers and hide.  If you wait out here in the open much longer your wish may come true. }{Murus Faralain}{}{}{}{1.000}{1}
E {20345}{se}{se}{6}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}
E {20346}{w}{w}{8}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}

R {20348}{0}{<238>}{-=\x7B* Torchstroll Bridge *\x7D=- (se,w)}{<238>===<088>}{By the road, larger edifices and shops that are mostly intact hide both treasures beyond the imagination and unimaginable death.  It is unsettling, as huge vines and leafy shrubs seem to crawl out of and obscure the openings in the buildings.  Strolling quietly down this road, you pause to examine the roadbed of loose bricks.  You stand on the ruins of a once magnificent white stone bridge.  Through holes in the ivy covered balustrades you see a stream flows north-south under it. The dense forest to the northeast comes almost to the bridge, while the stream flows off to the south through more open parkland dotted with ruined and abandoned buildings.   }{Murus Faralain}{}{}{}{1.000}{1}
