R {39027}{0}{<248>}{A Small Cave (s)}{C}{           A Small Cave. This is a small cave set in the vast mountain walls which lie to the north of Seahaven. And yet, a simple cave with rocky walls and a sandy floor it is not. This was clearly a very important place at one time as etched all over the walls and floor you see fantastic magical runes. On one wall is a remaining shelf, but it is empty, the contents long gone. The room is illuminated by a pure ray of sunlight which descends from an unknown source directly above. The light strikes a pure crystal placed in the exact center of the floor. On one wall is a strange stand which resembles a coat rack, although you guess that is probably not its use. There is a funny haze next to the rack into which you could peer. }{Seahaven}{}{}{}{1.000}{1}
E {39020}{s}{s}{4}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}
E {39028}{n}{n}{1}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}

R {39028}{0}{<248>}{In a Small Cave (n)}{<248>% %<088>}{       The fissure has emerged into a small cave and you can see an opening ahead through which daylight is pouring in. On the floor of the cave is pure white sand, probably composed of rock and seashell particulates. Somehow this cave reminds you of one you have seen before. Everything in here appears to have once come from the sea. What appears to have been a huge piece of coral now stands as a table to one side of the room. On the other side of the room is a huge clam shell. }{Seahaven}{}{}{}{1.000}{1}
E {39027}{s}{s}{4}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}
E {39029}{n}{n}{1}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}

R {39029}{0}{<248>}{The Opening to the Cave (s)}{P}{                   The Opening to the Cave. You stand at the mouth to the cave. Before you is a most remarkable and abstract view\; it was worth coming all this way just to have the opportunity to observe this wonder of the world. You are some distance above the floor of the valley below. The cave is a small pocket in a huge rock face which forms the foundations of the mountain next to which Seahaven is nestled. Way in the distance you see the snowy peaks and jagged ridges of broken and rugged mountains. Leading towards you from the mountains is a huge plateau of glaciers flowing like ribbons through the mountain valleys. Ice-falls and crevasses are visible even from here, clearly they would be absolutely vast in size if seen from up close. The most remarkable and fantastic thing is the ships. Poking from within the very ice itself is boat upon boat. Some have just the tip of the mast visible above the ice, while for others you can see the hull, and in some cases even the keel. It is fantastic to see vessels of the sea in amongst the mountain terrain where the seashore is but a distant memory, clearly tremendous forces have been at work here. A thin path angles down towards the base of the cliffs providing a difficult trail to the glacier below. }{Seahaven}{}{}{}{1.000}{1}
E {39028}{s}{s}{4}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}
E {39030}{climb down}{climb down}{0}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}

R {39030}{0}{}{On a Ship on the Ice (climb)}{<238>|||<088>}{         On a Ship on the Ice. You are on board a ship perched on the ice. It is snuggled up against a vast rock face which towers above you. The thin path which brought you here is easy to reach from the deck where you stand and climbs up the rock face weaving between ledges. The ship is old and decrepit, holes in the deck making getting around precarious. The cabin is gone, and through the holes you can see the hull is virtually a skeleton of what was once was without doubt a proud merchant ship. Surprisingly, just inboard of the transom, the wheel still proudly stands. }{Seahaven}{}{}{}{1.000}{1}
E {39031}{climb}{climb}{0}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}

