R {45459}{0}{}{Nicodemus Heights (s,e)}{ }{     This wooded hill west of Hagerstown Pike offers an excellent view of the northern portion of Antietam Battlefield.  Confederate artillery pounded Union infantry attempting to take the cornfield, about a half mile east of this hill.  Hagerstown Pike runs south, disappearing as it bends around a line of trees. }{Antietam Battlefield}{}{}{}{1.000}{1}
E {45457}{e}{e}{2}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}
E {45461}{s}{s}{4}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}

R {45460}{0}{}{Inside Dunker Church (leave)}{ }{      The Dunker Church is just as small inside as it appears outside.  Summer sunshine streams through paned windows, reflecting off of white plaster walls.  Arranged in a row are a dozen wooden pews\; comfort was a second thought for the original worshippers.  A simple wooden table is the only other thing you see within.  It is obvious that the Dunkers valued simplicity. }{Antietam Battlefield}{}{}{}{1.000}{1}
E {45431}{leave}{leave}{0}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}

R {45461}{0}{}{West Woods (e,n)}{ }{       The West Woods is a dense forest that provided cover for the Confederate defenders.  Infantry led by Stonewall Jackson protected the woods against an ill-planned attack by General Greene's Twelfth Corps.  In a clearing stands a granite monument, marking the area where the Union army suffered over 2000 casualties within an hour.  The West Woods extend north towards higher ground.  Between the trees to the east you glimpse a single-story white-walled building. }{Antietam Battlefield}{}{}{}{1.000}{1}
E {45431}{e}{e}{2}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}
E {45459}{n}{n}{1}{0}{}{1.000}{}{0.00}

