R {50102}{0}{}{A Very Messy Laboratory (n,sw)}{41}{                     What a disaster! You are in the middle of what could be a laboratory, if someone took the time to clean it up.  Overcrowded wooden wall shelves are piled high with lab equipment such as half-filled, unlabelled beakers, chipped test tubes, white latex gloves, various sized pipettes, white filter paper, Petri dishes both large and small, and many small boxes of unknown origin.  The cluttered lab bench is piled with coffee-stained textbooks, beat-up old computers, dirty CRT monitors covered in yellow sticky notes, lab notebooks that are undoubtedly filled with utter gibberish, pens of many colours, dull and broken pencils, rulers whose gradations are almost completely worn off, crumpled up papers, various offprint articles, half-full coffee mugs, enormous boxes of tissues, and an old rotary telephone.  The drab white walls are almost completely obscured by various cardboard and paper posters, colourful paper printouts, an almost completely filled whiteboard, no less than three calendars, all of which show different months, and a large white clock.  The floor is almost completely covered with brown paper bags, many large beaten-up cardboard boxes, worn, smelly chairs, holey shoes, and even someone's jacket.  What a mess!  The only remotely clean object in the room is a shiny windowed door in the back of the lab.  A new-looking LCD screen is piled on top of a cluster of CRT monitors.  A large, dust-free unopened cardboard box sits in the middle of the room. }{Museum}{}{}{}{1.000}{1}
