R {50137}{0}{}{A Grand Castle Hall (se,u)}{51}{               This room is one of the many grand halls within the castle Elsinore, home to our beloved Hamlet. The walls are paneled with mirrored glass and bordered with inlaid gilding. Between the panels the wall is divided in height by a low gilded chair rail. At intervals of several feet, ornate candelabras garnish the already fancy decoration of the room. Above the main entry way, a split staircase trails the walls down either side of the doorway, creating a strangely gabled effect. About halfway down, the staircases turn ninety degrees inward. They continue gracefully to the floor, framing the path into the room. The whole image is truly majestic. Solid wood chairs with padded upholstery and inlaid gilding are lined along the perimeter of the room, each awaiting an occupant to fulfill their designed function. Starkly contrasting black marble and alabaster tiles, each turned on a forty-five degree angle to create a diamond pattern, cover the floor in elegance. }{Museum}{51}{}{}{1.000}{1}
